WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.336
-Our next guest is a Golden
Globe and Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:05.638
you know from shows like
"Chernobyl," "Mad Men,"

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:07.006
"The Terror," and "The Crown."

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:09.108
He currently stars
in "Foundation."

00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:11.945
The season finale streams
November 19th on Apple TV+.

00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:13.480
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.982
-The two emerging powers of the
Outer Reach united in marriage.

00:00:16.982 --> 00:00:19.686
-Are you going to teach us our
own history now, dead man, huh?

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:22.655
The Thespin dog slit her throat
on their wedding bed.

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:24.590
-So the legend goes.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.792
-Are you suggesting our history
is wrong?

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:29.529
-History is written by
the victor,

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.599
and neither of you
seem to be winning.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:35.768
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Jared Harris.

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:37.236
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:45.178 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:53.119 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.420
Jared, welcome back.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.422
-Is it too soon? Is it alright?

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:58.424
To occupy the chair
once occupied by

00:00:58.424 --> 00:01:00.059
the sexiest man alive.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:01.994
Is it alright?
-I think so.

00:01:01.994 --> 00:01:05.431
I mean, it's very rare to sit
in the sexi--

00:01:05.431 --> 00:01:08.935
be the next person to sit in
the sexiest man's seat.

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:10.503
-I'm getting something.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.271
I'm getting something.
-We can see.

00:01:12.271 --> 00:01:14.407
By the way,
we can see it working.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.776
[ Laughter ]
-Thank you, Paul.

00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:19.612
-It's so lovely to see you,
as always.

00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:22.815
So, this is a -- you play
a psychohistorian in this film.

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.283
-Yeah.
-There's not a role

00:01:24.283 --> 00:01:26.885
you can't play, but when they
presented you psychohistorian,

00:01:26.885 --> 00:01:28.020
did you think, "This is what
I've been waiting for"?

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.190
-"What the hell does that mean?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.757
Yeah.

00:01:31.757 --> 00:01:33.726
-It's based on a famous
science-fiction book.

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:36.429
-Yeah, several books by
Isaac Asimov, yeah,

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.498
which were the kind of uber text
for a lot of science fiction

00:01:39.498 --> 00:01:42.402
that followed afterwards like
"Battlestar Galactica"

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:45.138
and "Star Wars"
and stuff like that, yeah.

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:49.141
-You have tended to be
in some darker material.

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:51.611
You were here talking about
"Chernobyl,"

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:53.579
which, if you don't know
the story, a little dark.

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:56.015
[ Laughter ]
-Yes.

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:58.584
-"The Terror," which I --
You know I bang the drum

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:00.619
for "The Terror,"
a limited series on AMC,

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:03.056
which, if people haven't seen,
go back once to "Terror."

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:04.757
Timeless.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:07.192
In this though, you know,
it's both, you know --

00:02:07.192 --> 00:02:09.128
things aren't going well, but
you are a little bit more

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:11.263
optimistic than maybe we've seen
you in previous things.

00:02:11.263 --> 00:02:14.099
-Yes, well, actually,
the character I play is a --

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:17.469
he's come up with a predictive
model for the future,

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:21.941
and although the future
he predicts is not one

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:25.978
that the Galactic Empire likes,
there is that -- he sees that

00:02:25.978 --> 00:02:28.781
there is a way towards
a positive ending,

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:31.583
but it's going to take
a thousand years to get there.

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:32.785
-We've also talked about
the fact

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:35.654
that your characters often die.

00:02:35.654 --> 00:02:37.857
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:39.859
This has happened to you again
in this show.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:41.660
-Yeah, it means you keep
having to look for a new job.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:42.862
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:44.530
You're always --
Your agent's always like,

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.199
"He's not available now,
but he might be soon."

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.167
-Pretty soon, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:51.503
-But you are -- we see you
there -- I believe we see you

00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:53.839
post-death there, because you
are -- this is a good one

00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:55.641
for you because you die
and you get to come back.

00:02:55.641 --> 00:02:59.278
-Yeah, well, it's 25,000 years
in the future, so all the ideas

00:02:59.278 --> 00:03:02.015
that we're playing with now
about being able to download

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:03.415
your consciousness
and everything of that,

00:03:03.415 --> 00:03:06.653
that's all taken
as a given in that world.

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:08.421
-Which is nice, because then,
you know,

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:09.956
you get to keep doing the work,
you know?

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:12.091
-Yeah.
Yeah, I mean -- I don't know.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.059
Would I like that? I mean,
you know, there's the whole

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:16.262
tactile feeling of being alive
and everything.

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:18.664
I think I'm kind of
rooted to that.

00:03:18.664 --> 00:03:21.066
-Yeah. You like the touch part
of it, but like, eh.

00:03:21.066 --> 00:03:23.102
-Yeah, touch, taste, feel --
yeah.

00:03:23.102 --> 00:03:25.204
-Not for me, no.
-No? Yeah.

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:27.507
-I just like being able to tell
people what I'm thinking.

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:28.741
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:31.477
You can just bring me back
and let me go.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.445
What about a --
-There's a part for you then.

00:03:33.445 --> 00:03:35.148
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:37.850
Do you ever think like, "Hey,
what is something I could do

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.119
where, you know, Jared Harris
could play somebody that

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.587
would maybe make it to the end
of the thing?"

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:44.122
Like a rom-com, a musical?

00:03:44.122 --> 00:03:47.060
-In fact, I just did a movie
with my brother Damian.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.695
He's a director, and my younger
brother as well, he's an actor.

00:03:49.695 --> 00:03:51.197
The three of us were in it
together,

00:03:51.197 --> 00:03:53.032
and I make it to the end
of that story.

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:54.266
-You do?
-Yeah!

00:03:54.266 --> 00:03:58.637
-So that's very sweet.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:00.873
To some degree,
it takes working with family.

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:04.343
-Basically, yeah. People who
want to keep you around, right.

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:07.513
-When were your early -- like
when you -- you started

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:09.682
a little bit later
I guess in this field,

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:13.920
and what were your early
auditioning stories like?

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.556
-Actually I do remember a story
where I auditioned for

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:19.925
Mel Gibson's "Hamlet,"

00:04:19.925 --> 00:04:24.196
and we went down to the studio
to do a screen test.

00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:26.799
We all had to -- We had to get
on a train to get there.

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:29.102
And when we get there,
there's a car to pick us up.

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:32.604
And I'm about a year out of
drama school,

00:04:32.604 --> 00:04:37.576
and quite coincidentally
two of my classmates

00:04:37.576 --> 00:04:38.711
from drama school were there.

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:41.813
They're auditioning for Laertes,
a good part.

00:04:41.813 --> 00:04:43.015
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:45.384
I'm auditioning for a part
that has no lines.

00:04:45.384 --> 00:04:46.752
Right?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:46.752 --> 00:04:49.522
So they've given me Horatio
to read.

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:51.924
We get into the car
that's going to take us there.

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:54.427
The driver pulls out
a clipboard, and he goes,

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:56.162
"I'll just make sure
I've got everybody here."

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:57.696
And he looks at the clipboard
and he goes,

00:04:57.696 --> 00:05:02.668
"Hmm, Jason Isaacs
reading Laertes. Okay.

00:05:02.668 --> 00:05:06.138
James Purefoy reading Laertes
as well.

00:05:06.138 --> 00:05:07.940
I've split you apart."

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:10.910
And he goes, "Jared Harris
reading Horatio

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.146
although he cannot be cast in
this part."

00:05:13.146 --> 00:05:15.881
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.881 --> 00:05:18.651
-What a terrible thing to hear
in front of your friends.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:20.552
-Right in front of my friends,
and I'd been lying to them

00:05:20.552 --> 00:05:22.488
going, "oh, I'm up for Horatio.
I'm up for Horatio."

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:23.789
[ Laughter ]

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:25.958
-What are you -- You've been on
the other side.

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:27.593
You've directed as well.

00:05:27.593 --> 00:05:29.796
And so you've auditioned actors.

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:32.632
What's some advice you would
give to young actors who think

00:05:32.632 --> 00:05:35.367
that there's something, I don't
know, to the interactions

00:05:35.367 --> 00:05:36.836
you have before you actually
start doing the work?

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:40.406
-I think, well, first of all,
when you go in to audition,

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:42.841
the first thing they want to
find out is, "Are you crazy?"

00:05:42.841 --> 00:05:43.910
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:45.644
-Because no one wants
to hire crazy.

00:05:45.644 --> 00:05:47.413
-Right.
-So basically they sort of

00:05:47.413 --> 00:05:49.815
chat to you a bit.

00:05:49.815 --> 00:05:51.917
Don't talk too much.

00:05:51.917 --> 00:05:53.886
That's what I think because what
I watched was --

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.889
I'd watch people who would chat
and then they would sort of --

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.025
they would go, "Oh, I know
somebody that you know"

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:01.060
to the showrunner
or the director.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.394
But you don't really know what

00:06:02.394 --> 00:06:04.263
that person thinks
of that person.

00:06:04.263 --> 00:06:05.998
And quite often what would
happen is

00:06:05.998 --> 00:06:08.367
they would say something about
"Oh, we know someone in common."

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:09.535
And when they went
out of the room,

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.304
they go, "God, I can't stand
that person."

00:06:11.304 --> 00:06:12.671
And now all they remember is

00:06:12.671 --> 00:06:14.674
that you're connected
to that person.

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:16.476
-Yeah.
-So say as little as possible.

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:17.643
-Say as little as possible.

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:19.378
-Get to the audition part
as quick as you can.

00:06:19.378 --> 00:06:21.213
That's my advice.

00:06:21.213 --> 00:06:23.349
-You obviously take the craft
very seriously.

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.284
So much so, explain to us all

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:27.687
why you broke into a zoo
after hours.

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:30.122
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:32.325
-You know...

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:35.461
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:39.298
Decisions that you make
late at night

00:06:39.298 --> 00:06:41.300
after too many pints of beer...

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.868
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:45.404
-If it ends in a zoo,
it's a bad decision, yeah.

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:46.772
[ Laughter ]

00:06:46.772 --> 00:06:49.141
I think it might be --
like, not just a couple pints.

00:06:49.141 --> 00:06:50.842
[ Laughter ]
-No, it was several.

00:06:50.842 --> 00:06:53.812
We were -- I was at drama school
and we were graduating,

00:06:53.812 --> 00:06:56.048
and one of the things
you have to do in drama school

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:59.318
in the second year is
you do animal study.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.520
So you go every week to the zoo.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.590
You study an animal,
then you come back to the class

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.326
and then you try
and mimic the animal

00:07:07.326 --> 00:07:08.961
and your observations
of the animal.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:12.131
And at the end of the term,
you have zoo day where everybody

00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:15.535
in the school and the teachers
come and you're in the gymnasium

00:07:15.535 --> 00:07:18.136
and everyone is there doing
their animals,

00:07:18.136 --> 00:07:20.339
so it's like they're visiting
the zoo, right?

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:23.576
[ Laughter ]
So I had --

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:25.711
The one that I had did
was a gorilla.

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:29.548
And I felt like I'd bonded with
this gorilla.

00:07:29.548 --> 00:07:33.920
And we were saying goodbye
to all of our friends

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.122
and everything, and I thought
we should go and say goodbye

00:07:37.122 --> 00:07:40.126
to our animals
who we had bonded with.

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:45.564
And so, yeah, we went to
the zoo, broke into the zoo.

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:48.367
[ Laughter ]

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:50.869
-How happy was the gorilla
to see you?

00:07:50.869 --> 00:07:52.771
-Well, they were asleep.
-Yeah, I would imagine.

00:07:52.771 --> 00:07:55.741
-Yeah, they were lying around.
We couldn't find them.

00:07:55.741 --> 00:08:00.746
Although at one point the guy
I was with decided to, like,

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:03.616
bullfight, if you like, with
a mountain goat,

00:08:03.616 --> 00:08:05.851
who aren't fooled
by the coat, you know?

00:08:05.851 --> 00:08:07.152
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:08:07.152 --> 00:08:09.388
-They're not fooled by that.
They go straight for you.

00:08:09.388 --> 00:08:10.890
-Right, yeah.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.559
Yeah, It's a mistake to think
just because a bull is fooled.

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:15.093
Two very different animals.

00:08:15.093 --> 00:08:18.330
-Completely. Their horns.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.298
-Well, we all do appreciate
how seriously you took it.

00:08:20.298 --> 00:08:21.600
And by the way,
the results show.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.035
-Thank you very much.

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:24.937
-And thanks so much for being
here. Congrats on the show.

00:08:24.937 --> 00:08:26.838
-Thank you again.
-Jared Harris, everybody.

00:08:26.838 --> 00:08:28.874
The season finale of
"Foundation" streams

00:08:28.874 --> 00:08:30.543
November 19th on Apple TV+.

